I have had a lot of pleasure from <a href="http://underscore.js" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" data-saferedirecturl="https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://underscore.js&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792410269289000&amp;usg=AOvVaw37RS6aFzf3b0zUpRo41rQZ">underscore.js</a> (Thanks Joe).  Not only does it attempt to solve some of the problems you want to solve, it is also it a good way to get clues on how ordinary javascript works.  <div><br></div><div>For example, map is an underscore function which iterates across a data structure, either an array or an object (there is not builtin map for objects)<br><br>In a browser debug console you can type the function name and see the internals.  <br><br><div><font face="courier new, monospace">&gt; _.map</font></div><div><font face="courier new, monospace">function (e, t, n) {var r=[];return p&amp;&amp;e.map===p?e.map(t,n):(N(e,function(e,i,s){r[r.length]=t.call(n,e,i,s)}),r);}</font></div><div><br></div><div>This is the minified version of underscore, hence the crummy short names.  Let&#39;s visit the <a href="http://underscorejs.org/underscore.js" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" data-saferedirecturl="https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://underscorejs.org/underscore.js&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792410269289000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1WZG4y4AjWkqs0-gIHxKiI">development version</a> to see it in &#39;human&#39;:</div><div><br></div><div><pre style="color:rgb(0,0,0);word-wrap:break-word;white-space:pre-wrap"><font face="courier new, monospace">  _.map = _.collect = function(obj, iterator, context) {
    var results = [];
    if (obj == null) return results;
    if (nativeMap &amp;&amp; obj.map === nativeMap) return obj.map(iterator, context);
    each(obj, function(value, index, list) {
      results[results.length] = iterator.call(context, value, index, list);
    });
    return results;
  };</font></pre></div><div><br></div><div>I wanted to see how object iteration worked, and it&#39;s delegated to &#39;each&#39; which is also an underscore function</div><div><br></div><div><font face="courier new, monospace">_.each= ...</font></div><div><pre style="color:rgb(0,0,0);word-wrap:break-word;white-space:pre-wrap"><font face="courier new, monospace">   for (var key in obj) {
        if (_.has(obj, key)) {
          if (iterator.call(context, obj[key], key, obj) === breaker) return;
        }
      }</font></pre><pre style="color:rgb(0,0,0);word-wrap:break-word;white-space:pre-wrap"><font face="courier new, monospace">...</font></pre></div><div><br></div><div>So it turns out it uses for (var key in obj) to iterate over an object; I&#39;ve learned something new about javascript.</div><div><br></div><div>-Alex</div></div>
